@node Overview
@chapter Overview

A decimal stamp such as @samp{09761.4564} names a moment: day 9761,
0.4564 of the way through it.  Unless stated otherwise, examples use the
default epoch and 86400-second length.  A duration is a fraction of a day, so
90 minutes is @samp{.0625} and a working day of eight hours is
@samp{.3333}.  Durations add as plain decimals.

@example
$ daykeep .9 - .1
.2000
$ daykeep --hm '09:00 - 10:30' 11:00 - 11:45
1h 30m
2h 15m
$ daykeep --now
09761.4564
@end example

daykeep also reads @samp{HH:MM} wall-clock times. Hours and minutes can be
printed as well (@pxref{Summing ranges}).

@node Decimal time
@chapter Decimal time

@menu
* The epoch::           Day 0 and why it is in UTC.
* Changing the epoch::  --epoch, DAYKEEP_EPOCH and the config file.
* Choosing day length:: --day-length, DAYKEEP_DAY_LENGTH and day_length.
* Units::               What the decimals mean.
* Printed forms::       How stamps and durations are printed.
@end menu

@node The epoch
@section The epoch

@cindex epoch
@cindex day 0
Day 0 is 2000-01-01, and it starts at 00:00 UTC, unless you choose
another epoch (@pxref{Changing the epoch}).  With the default 86400-second
length, day numbers count whole
days from there: day 1 is 2000-01-02, and day 9761 is 2026-09-22.
Days before the epoch have negative numbers.  In terms of Unix time:

@example
stamp = (unix_seconds - epoch_seconds) / day_length
@end example

@noindent
The defaults are @samp{epoch_seconds = 946684800} and
@samp{day_length = 86400}.  You can check the default current stamp
against @command{date} and @command{bc}:

@example
$ echo "scale=4; ($(date -u +%s) - $(date -u -d 2000-01-01 +%s)) / 86400" | bc
9761.4564
$ daykeep --now
09761.4564
@end example

@cindex UTC
@cindex time zone
A default day begins at midnight UTC, not at local midnight, so a stamp names
the same moment everywhere and never skips or repeats around daylight
saving changes.  The price is that a decimal day does not line up with
your local day.  West of Greenwich a new day number begins in the
evening (at 21:00 in UTC@minus{}3), and east of it one begins in the
morning.  Work that runs past that point ends on the next day number,
and daykeep handles that as it handles work past local midnight
(@pxref{Ranges}).

@node Changing the epoch
@section Changing the epoch

@cindex epoch, changing
@cindex configuration file
Day 0 can be any moment.  daykeep takes the first of these that is set:

@enumerate
@item
the @option{--epoch=@var{date}} option;
@item
the @env{DAYKEEP_EPOCH} environment variable;
@item
an @samp{epoch = @var{date}} line in the configuration file,
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/daykeep/config}, or
@file{~/.config/daykeep/config} when @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} is unset or
not an absolute name;
@item
2000-01-01 00:00 UTC.
@end enumerate

@var{date} is written as @option{--convert} reads a date
(@pxref{Now and convert}), but without an offset it is UTC, not local
time, so it names the same moment wherever daykeep runs.  An offset
makes days start at another hour; this configuration file starts them
at midnight in UTC@minus{}3, and keeps the numbers of an older epoch
in a comment:

@example
# ~/.config/daykeep/config
# epoch = 1987-07-28
epoch = 2000-01-01 00:00 -0300
@end example

In the file, blank lines and lines starting with @samp{#} are ignored,
and blanks around the key and the value do not matter.  The settings are
@samp{epoch} and @samp{day_length}; if either appears twice, its last
value counts.  A file
that does not exist is fine, but a line daykeep cannot read, or a file
it cannot open, is an error (exit status 2).  Each setting independently
uses its option, then its nonempty environment variable, then the file,
then its default.  The file is read when either setting needs it.
Overridden values of known keys are ignored; unknown keys and malformed
lines remain errors.  Override both settings to bypass the file entirely.
If an option is repeated, its last value counts.

@cindex log, and the epoch
Stamps count from the epoch, so a log only sums correctly with the
epoch and day length it was written with.  Durations do not depend on
the epoch, but their decimal representation depends on day length.  If you move
the epoch, keep the old one in @env{DAYKEEP_EPOCH} or
@option{--epoch} when reading old logs, or convert them first.

@node Choosing day length
@section Choosing day length

@cindex day length
Earth's rotation is the default, not a requirement.  Choose a personal
day with @option{--day-length=@var{seconds}}, @env{DAYKEEP_DAY_LENGTH},
or @samp{day_length = @var{seconds}} in the configuration file, in that
order.  The default is 86400 seconds.  Any whole number from 1 through
1000000000 is accepted, written with decimal digits only.  Zero, signs,
fractions, unit suffixes and larger values are errors (exit status 2).

@example
# ~/.config/daykeep/config
epoch = 2000-01-01 03:00 -0300
day_length = 90000

$ daykeep --day-length=90000 --hm .9 - .1
5h 0m
@end example

The epoch stays fixed.  Day @var{n} starts exactly @var{n} times
@var{day_length} seconds after it, without adjusting for midnight,
time zones or daylight saving.  The chosen length applies to stamps,
decimal durations, completion, rollover, conversions and tracking.
Civil dates, @samp{HH:MM}, and hours and minutes retain their usual
meaning.  In particular, @samp{HH:MM} means that time on the civil
date of the reference instant, even when a personal day has another length.

Logs carry neither the epoch nor the length.  Read or resume old logs
with their original settings, using @option{--epoch} and
@option{--day-length} if your defaults have changed.

@node Units
@section Units

daykeep keeps every value as a whole number of seconds.  Decimal input
rounds to whole seconds, half up; arithmetic then uses those seconds,
and output rounds half up to four decimal places.  One displayed step
is @var{day_length}/10000 seconds.  For lengths below 10000 seconds,
some typed fractions cannot be represented exactly: with a one-second
day, @samp{.5} rounds to one full day.

With the default length, one step is 8.64 seconds and some common
durations are:

@multitable {15 minutes} {1.0000} {no (.000694@dots{})}
@headitem Duration @tab Decimal @tab Exact?
@item 1 minute    @tab .0007 @tab no (.000694@dots{})
@item 15 minutes  @tab .0104 @tab no (.010416@dots{})
@item 1 hour      @tab .0417 @tab no (.041666@dots{})
@item 90 minutes  @tab .0625 @tab yes
@item 3 hours     @tab .1250 @tab yes
@item 6 hours     @tab .2500 @tab yes
@item 8 hours     @tab .3333 @tab no (.333333@dots{})
@item 1 day       @tab 1.0000 @tab yes
@end multitable

A duration of @var{n} minutes is @var{n} times 60 divided by
@var{day_length} days.  With the default length, it is @var{n}/1440 day.
Whole multiples
of 18 minutes (@samp{.0125}) print exactly, as 90 minutes and 3 hours
do.  Other durations are rounded to the nearest step, half up.

@node Printed forms
@section Printed forms

@itemize
@item
A stamp is printed with a day number of at least five digits and four
decimals: @samp{09761.4564}, @samp{00007.0000}.  Every stamp from day
0 on reads back as exactly that stamp, without completion (@pxref{Stamps}),
provided its rounded value fits signed 64-bit seconds.  Day numbers may
contain up to 19 digits; values exceeding that seconds range are rejected.
A moment before the epoch is printed as its negative day number plus
the fraction into that day: @samp{-0001.1250} is 03:00 UTC on day
@minus{}1, 1999-12-31.  Such stamps are printed but not read.

@item
A duration under one day is printed without a leading zero:
@samp{.0625}.  Longer ones print the whole days: @samp{1.2500}.

@item
@option{--hm} prints durations as @samp{1h 30m}, and
@option{--format=minutes} as a whole number of minutes, @samp{90}.
@end itemize

@node Stamps
@chapter Stamps

@cindex stamp
@cindex completion
A stamp is written @samp{@var{day}.@var{fraction}}, and either side may
be short.  daykeep fills in what is missing.

@table @asis
@item Missing right digits
are zeros.  @samp{.5} is @samp{.5000}, and @samp{9761} is
@samp{09761.0000}.

@item Missing left digits
are taken from today.  The digits you give are the last digits of the
day number, and daykeep picks the @emph{most recent} day, up to today,
whose number ends in them.  With no day digits at all, the day is
today.

@item A leading @samp{+}
picks the @emph{first} day from today on that ends in those digits
instead.
@end table

With today being day 9761:

@multitable {@samp{+761.25}} {09761.2500}
@headitem Typed @tab Means
@item @samp{.5}       @tab 09761.5000
@item @samp{761}      @tab 09761.0000
@item @samp{9}        @tab 09759.0000
@item @samp{762}      @tab 08762.0000
@item @samp{+9}       @tab 09769.0000
@item @samp{+761.25}  @tab 09761.2500
@item @samp{+762}     @tab 09762.0000
@item @samp{9750.1}   @tab 09750.1000
@item @samp{0761}     @tab 00761.0000
@end multitable

A stamp with as many day digits as today's number, or more, is taken
literally, so a printed stamp reads back as itself.

@cindex HH:MM
@cindex local time
A stamp may also be written @samp{HH:MM}, meaning that time on your
local clock today, as set by @env{TZ}.  With @option{-u} it is read
in UTC instead.

@node Ranges
@chapter Ranges

@cindex range
A range is written @samp{@var{start} - @var{end}}.  The @samp{-} is
required; spaces around it are optional.

@var{start} is completed from today, as any stamp (@pxref{Stamps}).
@var{end} is completed from @var{start}: when it leaves out day digits,
it is the first matching time that is not before @var{start}.  So an
entry that runs past the start of a day number needs no day digits:

@example
$ daykeep .9 - .1
.2000
$ daykeep 9761.375 - .4375
.0625
$ daykeep --hm 23:24 - 00:52
1h 28m
@end example

@noindent
In the first example, @samp{.1} would fall before @samp{.9} on the same
day, so it rolls on to the next day.  An @var{end} with a full day
number is taken literally, and it is an error for it to fall before
@var{start}.

A range whose @var{end} is missing, @samp{@var{start} -} at the end of a
line, is still open.  It runs until now, which is how an entry that the
tracker is still timing is summed.

@node Summing ranges
@chapter Summing ranges

With no mode option, daykeep sums ranges.  They come from:

@enumerate
@item
the operands, joined by spaces, so a range may span several of them
(@samp{daykeep 10:00 - 11:00} works); then
@item
each @option{-f @var{file}}, in order (@samp{-} is standard input);
@item
standard input, if there are neither operands nor files.
@end enumerate

A line may hold several ranges.  After each range daykeep prints the
running total.  With @option{-s} it prints only the final total, even
if there were no ranges at all.

@example
$ cat today.log
09761.3750 - 09761.4375
09761.4583 - 09761.5000
$ daykeep -f today.log
.0625
.1042
$ daykeep -s --hm < today.log
2h 30m
@end example

@cindex errors
A line that cannot be read is reported on standard error, prefixed with
@samp{@var{file}:@var{line}:} when it came from a file, and the rest of
that line is skipped.  Summing goes on, and daykeep exits with status 1
at the end.

@node Now and convert
@chapter Now and convert

@option{--now} prints the current stamp.

@option{--convert} (@option{-c}) converts each @var{value}:

@itemize
@item
A decimal stamp becomes a local date and time, in a form that
@option{--convert} reads back:
@example
$ TZ=America/Sao_Paulo daykeep -c 9761.5
2026-09-22 09:00:00 -0300
@end example

@item
@samp{HH:MM}, or an ISO date
@samp{YYYY-MM-DD[ HH:MM[:SS]][ +HHMM]}, becomes a stamp.  A date with
no offset is local time.
@example
$ daykeep -c 2000-01-01 '2026-09-22 12:00 +0000'
00000.1250
09761.5000
@end example
@end itemize

@noindent
(The first result is for a zone at UTC@minus{}3, where local midnight
is 03:00 UTC.)  With @option{-u}, dates and times are read and printed
in UTC.

@node Tracker
@chapter Tracker

@cindex tracker
@option{--track} (@option{-t}) times your work interactively.  It shows
a table of entries and a running total:

@example
$ daykeep -t -a work.log
 1  09761.3750 - 09761.4375   .0625
 2  09761.4564 - ...          (running)
total .0625
[enter] stamp  [x] remove last  [e] edit last  [s] save  [q] quit
@end example

@table @asis
@item @key{RET}
Stamp the current time.  Stamps start and end entries in turn.  An
entry whose start was edited into the future ends at its start, so no
entry ends before it starts; edit the end afterwards.
@item @kbd{x}
Remove the last stamp.
@item @kbd{e}
Edit the last stamp.  It takes any stamp form (@samp{.4},
@samp{14:30}, @samp{9761.4}).  The first stamp is completed from
today, and later ones from the stamp before, as a range @var{end} is,
so @samp{.9} then @samp{.1} ends on the next day.
@item @kbd{s}
Save the log (it is kept current anyway, see below).
@item @kbd{q}, @kbd{C-c}, @kbd{C-d}
Quit.
@end table

@cindex log
With @option{-a @var{log}}, the session's entries are kept in
@var{log}, one range per line, as @samp{@var{start} - @var{end}}, or
@samp{@var{start} -} while an entry is open.  daykeep rewrites the
session's lines after every change, so the file is always current: a
signal or a crash loses nothing.  Lines that were in @var{log} before
the session are never touched.

If @var{log} ends in an open entry, the next @samp{daykeep -t -a
@var{log}} resumes it.  Only one tracker at a time can use a given
@var{log}.  Because the lines are ranges, @samp{daykeep -f @var{log}}
sums the log, open entry included.

Without @option{-a}, nothing is written to disk.

@node Invoking daykeep
@chapter Invoking daykeep

@example
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} [@var{range}]@dots{}
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --now
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --convert @var{value}@dots{}
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --track
@end example

@table @option
@item -f @var{file}
@itemx --file=@var{file}
Read ranges from @var{file}; @samp{-} is standard input.  May be given
more than once.

@item -s
@itemx --summarize
Print only the final total.

@item --format=@var{fmt}
Print durations as @var{fmt}: @samp{decimal} (@samp{.0625}, the
default), @samp{hm} (@samp{1h 30m}) or @samp{minutes} (@samp{90}).

@item --hm
Same as @option{--format=hm}.

@item --now
Print the current stamp.

@item -c
@itemx --convert
Convert each operand (@pxref{Now and convert}).

@item -t
@itemx --track
Track time interactively (@pxref{Tracker}).

@item -a @var{log}
@itemx --append=@var{log}
With @option{--track}, keep the entries in @var{log}.

@item -u
@itemx --utc
Read and print wall-clock times in UTC instead of local time.

@item --epoch=@var{date}
Count days from @var{date} instead of 2000-01-01 00:00 UTC
(@pxref{Changing the epoch}).

@item --day-length=@var{seconds}
Choose the number of whole seconds in a personal day, from 1 through
1000000000; the default is 86400 (@pxref{Choosing day length}).

@item --help
Print a usage message and exit.

@item --version
Print version information and exit.
@end table

Options may be mixed with operands unless @env{POSIXLY_CORRECT} is set,
and @samp{--} ends the options.

@section Exit status

@table @asis
@item 0
All went well.
@item 1
Some input was invalid; the rest was still processed.
@item 2
A usage error, or an I/O error (including a failed write to standard
output).
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item ~/.config/daykeep/config
Settings; @pxref{Changing the epoch}.
@end table

@section Environment

@table @env
@item TZ
The time zone for @samp{HH:MM} and for dates, unless @option{-u} is
given.
@item POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must come before operands.
@item DAYKEEP_EPOCH
Day 0, unless @option{--epoch} is given (@pxref{Changing the epoch}).
@item DAYKEEP_DAY_LENGTH
Seconds per personal day, unless @option{--day-length} is given
(@pxref{Choosing day length}).  An empty value counts as unset.
@item XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration file is looked for, as
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/daykeep/config}; @file{~/.config} if unset.
@item DAYKEEP_NOW
If set, used as the current time: a full stamp such as
@samp{09761.4564}, or @samp{@@@var{seconds}} since the Unix epoch.  It
is meant for tests.
@end table

@node Other tools
@chapter Using daykeep with other tools

Decimal output is plain numbers, so the usual tools can work with it.

@itemize
@item
Sum several logs in minutes with @command{awk}:
@example
$ for f in *.log; do daykeep -s --format=minutes -f "$f"; done |
  awk '@{ m += $1 @} END @{ printf "%dh %02dm\n", m / 60, m % 60 @}'
@end example

@item
Show today's total in a tmux status line, or mail it from
@command{cron}:
@example
set -g status-right '#(daykeep -s -f ~/work.log)'
@end example

@item
Check a stamp against @command{date}, using the default epoch and length:
@example
$ date -d @@$(( (9761 + 10957) * 86400 ))
@end example
@end itemize

@bye
